{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-9263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd45c604be785a4cb8d791de7709ff54f6180db9c87ae17f485093ed2549b51a"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-2,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd45c604be785a4cb8d791de7709ff54f6180db9c87ae17f485093ed2549b51a"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-9264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd45c604be785a4cb8d791de7709ff54f6180db9c87ae17f485093ed2549b51a"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{15-12}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd45c604be785a4cb8d791de7709ff54f6180db9c87ae17f485093ed2549b51a"} {"question" "q-85170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014369/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f0a3007555286951023e9ecba7934ecb3eff43ad1a0189a53feb7b91a9d8c65"} {"question" "q-85170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math